  "output": {
    "stories": [
      {
        "editor_spans": [
          {
            "body": "Burnham's case for devolution rests on a story about Ravenscraig and Leigh. Distant people made decisions without facing the consequences, and nobody planned what came next. But in his own speech, the place where that is happening right now is Aberdeen. He puts it off with 'more to say in due course' and then moves on. Grangemouth gets one sentence about 'working with the Scottish government'. The decisions that will shape the North Sea's decline are about licensing, energy taxes and the pace of the transition. None of them is in his offer. What he would devolve is employment support, innovation funding and skills, which are the tools for dealing with the aftermath. The decisions that caused Ravenscraig's closure would stay where they were. If the lesson of the 1990s is that nobody asked the community what came next, the test comes now, while the work is still there, not after it has gone.",
            "cites": [
              81,
              82,
              84,
              92,
              108,
              183,
              185,
              299,
              305
            ]
          },
          {
            "body": "The one concrete offer, devolving DWP employment support, comes with conditions. Burnham says it will be 'shaped by Holyrood'. He also says it 'has to go deep', must be built on 'the Easterhouse principle', and that 'if it's not based on that, it won't work'. So Westminster would hand over a power and set the design rules in the same breath. That is the same top-down approach he criticises elsewhere in the speech. There is a deeper tension too. Easterhouse worked because it was small, run by people on one estate, and built on personal trust. Turning that into 'the way we support everybody, everywhere' makes it a national programme. That is exactly the kind of scheme he says 'won't work again'. When bottom-up projects are rolled out nationally, they usually turn into the tick-box systems they were meant to replace. The speech never asks how to stop that happening.",
            "cites": [
              49,
              51,
              238,
              239,
              306,
              308,
              310,
              313,
              315,
              316
            ]
          },
          {
            "body": "The bus story is meant to show that local control works. It also shows what Burnham's reindustrialisation really depends on: paying 'even a little more' to buy British. Applied to 'all public procurement in the UK', that is a national industrial policy. Procurement and trade rules are set at the centre, and in the same speech he is negotiating a new deal with the EU. So the strongest lever in his plan is one that cannot be devolved. It would be pulled from Whitehall, by the same centre he says should let go.",
            "cites": [
              190,
              195,
              197,
              198,
              201
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
